
# 1. CONTROLS SEQUENCE OF OPERATION

**Make-Up Air Unit (MAU)**

---

## 1.1 GENERAL CONTROL REQUIREMENTS

1. The Make-Up Air Unit (MAU) shall be controlled by a dedicated local controller networked to the Building Automation System (BAS).
2. All control logic and programming shall reside locally in the MAU controller. The unit shall continue to operate autonomously upon loss of BAS network communications.
3. The steam generator shall be controlled by the manufacturer’s integral controller and shall communicate to the BAS via BACnet/IP for monitoring and enable/disable commands only.
4. All adjustable control setpoints shall be operator-adjustable via the BAS.
5. Space temperature, space relative humidity, and space differential pressure setpoints shall be as defined in the Room Criteria Schedule.

---

## 1.2 SYSTEM ENABLE / DISABLE

1. The MAU system shall be enabled at all times unless disabled by:

   * Safety interlock
   * Manual disconnect
   * Maintenance Mode (Section 1.10)
2. When enabled, one air handler shall operate as the active unit per the Lead / Standby sequence.

---

## 1.3 LEAD / STANDBY OPERATION

1. The MAU system shall consist of two parallel air handlers configured in an **N+1 lead / standby arrangement**.
2. Only one air handler shall operate at a time under normal conditions.
3. The lead air handler assignment shall rotate automatically on a weekly basis.
4. Upon detection of a lead unit failure, the system shall:

   * Command the standby unit to start
   * Annunciate a lead unit failure alarm to the BAS
5. Prior to starting the standby unit:

   * The CHW cooling coil valve and HW preheat coil valve on the standby unit shall be commanded fully open
   * Valves shall remain fully open for **2 minutes** to purge and prime the coils
6. After the standby unit is confirmed running, valve control shall revert to normal automatic modulation.

---

## 1.4 FAN AND DAMPER SEQUENCE

### 1.4.1 Damper Control

1. When an air handler is enabled to operate:

   * The outdoor air damper shall be commanded open
   * The supply air damper shall be commanded open
2. Damper open position shall be proven via end switch prior to fan enable.

---

### 1.4.2 Fan Operation

1. Each air handler shall consist of an **N-1 fan array**.
2. During normal operation, all fans in the array shall be commanded ON.
3. Fan speed shall modulate to maintain the **space differential pressure setpoint**. 

---

### 1.4.3 Fan Failure Response

1. Fan failure shall be detected via:

   * VFD status feedback, or
   * Current transducer (CT) input
2. Upon failure of any fan:

   * Remaining fans shall increase speed as required to maintain the space differential pressure setpoint
   * A fan failure alarm shall be generated in the BAS

---

## 1.5 HEATING MODE

### 1.5.1 Heating Mode Enable

Heating Mode shall be active when Cooling Mode and Dehumidification Mode are not active.

---

### 1.5.2 Preheat Coil Control

1. The hot water preheat coil shall maintain a **Leaving Air Temperature (LAT) minimum of 45°F at all times**.
2. The preheat valve shall modulate to maintain the active Supply Air Temperature (SAT) setpoint.

---

### 1.5.3 Supply Air Temperature Reset (Heating)

1. During Heating Mode, the SAT setpoint shall reset linearly based on the position of the associated space sensible cooling coil valves:

   * When all associated sensible cooling coil valves are **100% closed**, SAT setpoint = **60°F**
   * When all associated sensible cooling coil valves are **100% open**, SAT setpoint = **45°F**

---

### 1.5.4 Reheat Coil Control

1. If the SAT setpoint cannot be maintained by the preheat coil alone:

   * The hot water reheat coil valve shall modulate open
   * Reheat valve modulation shall maintain the SAT setpoint

---

## 1.6 HUMIDIFICATION MODE

### 1.6.1 Humidification Enable

Humidification Mode shall be enabled when **either** of the following conditions are true for more than **5 minutes**:

* Outside air dewpoint is below **46°F**
* Space relative humidity is below **40%**

---

### 1.6.2 Humidification Operation

1. Upon Humidification Mode enable:

   * Steam generator and dispersion grid system shall be enabled
   * Supply air temperature setpoint shall be reset to **60°F**
2. Airflow shall be proven via airflow switch prior to humidifier enable.
3. The humidifier shall modulate to maintain the space relative humidity setpoint.

---

### 1.6.3 Humidification Disable

1. The steam generator system shall be placed in standby for **24 hours** when outside air dewpoint rises above **48°F for more than 5 minutes**.

---

## 1.7 COOLING MODE

### 1.7.1 Cooling Mode Enable

Cooling Mode shall be enabled when **both** of the following conditions are met for more than **5 minutes**:

* Outside air temperature > **50°F**
* Outside air dewpoint < **47°F**

---

### 1.7.2 Cooling Operation

1. The cooling coil control valve shall modulate to maintain a SAT setpoint of **47.5°F**.

---

### 1.7.3 Cooling Mode Disable

Cooling Mode shall be disabled when outside air temperature drops below **48°F for more than 5 minutes**.

---

## 1.8 DEHUMIDIFICATION MODE

### 1.8.1 Dehumidification Enable

Dehumidification Mode shall be enabled when **either** of the following conditions occur:

* Outside air dewpoint rises above **47°F for more than 5 minutes**
* Space relative humidity increases by more than **2% for more than 1 minute**

---

### 1.8.2 Dehumidification Operation

1. Cooling coil valve shall modulate to maintain a **Leaving Air Temperature (LAT) setpoint of 47.5°F**.
2. Reheat coil valve shall modulate to maintain the active SAT setpoint.

---

### 1.8.3 Supply Air Temperature Reset (Dehumidification)

1. SAT setpoint shall reset linearly based on the position of the associated space sensible cooling coil valves:

   * Valves fully closed → SAT = **60°F**
   * Valves fully open → SAT = **45°F**

---

## 1.9 MODE PRIORITY

Mode priority shall be as follows (highest to lowest):

1. Safety shutdowns
2. Maintenance Mode
3. Dehumidification Mode
4. Humidification Mode
5. Cooling Mode
6. Heating Mode

---

## 1.10 MAINTENANCE MODE

1. A Maintenance Mode override shall be provided in the BAS front-end graphics for each air handler tunnel.
2. When Maintenance Mode is activated for a unit:

   * The selected unit shall shut down
   * The standby unit shall start via the normal lead / standby changeover sequence
3. No alarms shall be generated for a unit while in Maintenance Mode.
4. Maintenance Mode shall remain active until manually canceled by the operator.
5. A BAS alarm shall be generated if a unit remains in Maintenance Mode longer than **24 hours**.

